hey guys I just got out of doing a  little segment in Kitchener were just  like two hours away from my home in  Toronto and we're just taking a little  little coffee break Starbucks break and  I thought I would address I some some  controversy around sponsorship because  there's definitely been a lot of  misconceptions around sponsorship and  like conspiracy theories about  dieticians being shills for industry and  things like that and you know I love to  be super transparent with you guys about  everything and so I thought I would give  you a little bit of behind-the-scenes  insight into how exactly sponsorship  works for dietitian content creators  like myself so first of all let's talk  about how things work over Abby's  kitchen as a general rule of thumb I  keep my sponsored content to no more  than 25% of my total content calendar  across all my platform is YouTube you  know my blog my Instagram etc TV  magazines whatever and so all of my  content both my sponsored and unspun  sered I make decisions about what I want  to post based on a few really simple  pieces of criteria one what am I really  excited about and passionate about right  now and two what is it that my audience  is asking for now if you go over to my  blog Abbie's kitchen you'll see I cover  a lot of different things more much  wider range I would say then then I  cover on my channel here because I have  such a more diverse audience I think on  my blog I have a lot of different  interests and also my audience are very  inquisitive and they have a lot of  interests as well yeah like the mom  recipes and the baby recipes may not  apply to you if you're like past that  mom's age or you're not a mom or you're  not a parent you're not feeding little  ones but you know the vegan recipes  might not apply to that busy mom who's  just like struggling to feed her picky  toddler who only wants to eat grilled  cheese so here at Abby's kitchen I  really try to be inclusive and  ultimately create really valuable  content for everyone and anyone who  loves to eat and who loves food now the  way it usually works is that I will  reach out to a brand that I really  admire and kind of talk about working on  a specific piece of content with them or  a brand wants to work with me  because we have similar philosophies and  those are aligned and therefore they  will reach out either directly to me or  to my agent talk about that  collaboration in the case of the latter  when a brain is reaching out to me  there's almost always a very specific  product campaign or piece of content  that they're looking at collaborating on  so it's really up to me to determine  whether or not that product is something  that my my philosophy and and my  expertise is well aligned with now if I  don't already know this brand really  well which is rare because I love to eat  and I love trying new products and I get  new things all the time to check out but  in the case if I don't I'm not really  familiar with the product the first step  is I need to try any to evaluate it and  trust me there are a lot of brands that  I say no shoot no matter how much money  they offer simply because it does not  past this initial kind of sniff test  that the taste test or the nutritional  test one that really stands out to me as  an example is Splenda you know I don't  use Splenda and I don't think it has a  whole lot of nutritive value and so even  though they've reached out to me on a  number of occasions for a number of  campaigns I've declined because it just  doesn't work for my you know my it  doesn't fit in within my philosophies  it's not well aligned with my brand so I  don't feel comfortable promoting it I  also want to flag that and acknowledge  that you know healthy for one person is  is not going to be healthy for another  because I have such a diverse audience  watching my content and I really try to  be inclusive of that and ultimately  healthy is not like a black and white  concept and as a dietitian my goal is to  really help to try to meet people where  they are so yes of course like a  convenience product may not be as  quote-unquote healthy as cooking that  that food from scratch however if it  helps a family have a relatively  balanced meal together as a family I  think that's a huge step in the right  direction and definitely something worth  promoting so like I said it's really  about meeting people where they are in  their journey towards better health so  next I want to talk about the actual  content itself of course brands will  have some things that they want me to  get across however I am super vocal  about exactly how I want to incorporate  a brand to my content and  exactly you know how I want to talk  about it all of my content is 100%  evidence-based and well aligned with my  personal and professional opinions it is  not fed to me by a brand hard stop  so no dieticians are not just puppets  for industry in fact I would never say  that a certain food is essential for  health and I would never claim that you  know you need to follow one type of diet  in order to be healthy I really try to  be inclusive and I think that the brands  that I work with really reflect that so  one week I might be working with a  yogurt brand and then the next week of  course I might be working with a you  know vegan pho meat company I have no  agenda here as long as it meets that  that basic criteria of you know my  nutritional and taste tests I also want  to flag that you know despite a lot of  like conspiracy theories and things like  that that are floating around the  messaging that I get from brands is  never extreme it's not it's usually  something like yogurt is rich in calcium  and probiotics or something like that  it's totally benign and in fact based  ultimately in fact a lot of the brands  and commodity boards that I work with  they have very strict marketing  guidelines that they need to abide by so  for example when I work with the almond  Board I'm not even allowed to say  something like almonds are healthy so  you can see like they're really really  strict and almonds obviously are rich in  healthy fats and things like that so it  makes sense that that is something that  would come out of my mouth  however those kinds of statements are  not allowed and that brings me to  another really important point and that  is what sponsorship is not so when I  partner with a brand for a sponsored  content it is for a very specific  campaign with very specific messages and  a very specific product it's not forever  more it doesn't not work like that I  mean finally I want to talk a little  about disclosure here folks now I am a  stickler for disclosure so anytime I  work with a brand that relationship is  always disclosed so the FTC which is the  Federal Trade Commission which aims to  protect the public from you know false  advertising and market  campaigns and things like that have  stated that any influencer working with  a brand needs to disclose the  relationship verbally in a YouTube video  at the beginning and at the end of the  video they needs to be in the  description at the top of the video so  that people can see it without having to  scroll it needs to be you know you'd say  sponsored or ad in any social media post  you need to tag the brand and use the  branded content tool I do all of these  things and I do them religiously so if  you're watching a YouTube video and a  person just starts talking about a brand  like you know halfway through the video  and they haven't disclosed in the first  30 seconds of the video and there's no  disclosure that the video is sponsored  in the video description itself that  person is breaking the rules and  honestly they can get in a lot of  trouble for that not to mention it's  just totally dishonest when it comes to  your relationship with your viewers and  your in your and your fans so this is a  person that you should ultimately be  distrustful of so next I want to talk  about my my content and also my brand  because any of the content that I put  out there needs to be grounded in  evidence and also in my own personal and  professional philosophy just because I  support a plant-based diet does not mean  that I can't also support a diet that  contains animal products meat and dairy  because guess what both can be healthy  and balanced or both can be unhealthy  and unbalanced as a lot of my what I in  today reviews have shown that isn't  contradictory that is me being inclusive  and ultimately that is my brand so I  would never say hey one week chicken is  essential for building muscle and then  the next week tell you that chicken is  bad you've never heard me call it a food  as being bad and then the next week  promote it because I was being paid to  that just it's never happened and it  won't ever happen so ultimately I never  contradict myself with sponsored and  unspun serd content because ultimately  my brand is very clear all foods can fit  I support people with a wide range of  dietary patterns and and I'm saying all  the time you know you do you plant-based  carnivore something in between a  combination whatever works for you works  for me personally I know like I  have a bit of like a flexitarian diet if  you want to label it but I've been very  open and hopefully as an bias as  possible talking about the benefits of  plant-based a plant-based diet and I've  talked about that here I've talked about  that and like legit every single video  I've also been very open and honest  about the fact that I am NOT vegan so my  brand isn't vegan my brand is an even  flexitarian my brand is a evidence-based  dietitian who wholeheartedly believes  that all foods can fit what feels good  to one person won't feel good to another  person and that is totally okay  diversity is beautiful it really is even  with diets if you go on my blog you'll  also notice there's a ton of unspun serd  vegan recipes and the reason for that is  because they satisfy those two pieces of  criteria that I talked about earlier  this is something that I'm interested in  because I'm excited about eating more  plant-based and this is something that a  lot of my audience is interested in as  well that's it and if you don't like  vegan recipes that's why I also create a  lot of non vegan recipes things like the  casseroles and the sheet pan meals and  the things that are great for for  families because that is another popular  request and also something that I'm into  as well here's the bottom line a  responsible influencer does a few really  important things one responds to her  audience and their requests and let me  tell you I get a diverse set of requests  too it really stays true to her own or  his own brand both with sponsored and on  sponsored content and three is  transparent honest and in my case as a  dietitian evidence-based that is my goal  here at Abby's kitchen and I will  continue to fight to uphold it so if you  guys have a very specific question that  you want me to focus on leave me a  comment below as you know as I've talked  about right here this channel is not  just about me it's really about you guys  and what you want to hear and see so  leave me comments questions this is all  what informs all of my content don't  forget to like this video subscribe  share it and I will see you next time on  Abby's kitchen bye  [Music]
 

